{{also|Bonnet}}
==English==

===Etymology===
{{rfscript|Gothic}}
From {{etyl|enm}} {{term|bonet|lang=enm}}, from {{etyl|frm}} {{term|bonet|lang=frm}} (Modern {{etyl|fr|-}} {{term|bonnet|lang=fr}}), from {{etyl|fro}} {{term|bonet||material from which hats are made|lang=fro}}, of {{etyl|gem}} origin, from {{etyl|frk}} *{{term||bunni|that which is bound|lang=frk}}, from {{proto|Germanic|bundian|bundle|lang=en}}, from {{proto|Germanic|bhendh-|to tie|lang=en}}. Compare also {{etyl|LL.|-}} {{term|abbonis|lang=la}}, {{term|obbonis||ribbon of a headdress|lang=la}}, also of {{etyl|gem|-}} origin, from {{etyl|frk|-}} *{{term||obbunni|lang=frk}}, from *{{term||ob-|above, over|lang=frk}} + *{{term||bunni|lang=frk}}. Cognate with {{etyl|goh|-}} {{term|gibunt||band, ribbon|lang=goh}}, {{etyl|dum|-}} {{term|bont||bundle, truss|lang=dum}}, {{etyl|got|-}} {{term|||tr=gabundi|bond|lang=got}}. More at {{l|en|over}}, {{l|en|bundle}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/bɒnət/}}
* {{audio|en-us-bonnet.ogg|Audio (US)}}

* {{rhymes|ɒnɪt}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A type of [[hat]], once worn by [[women]] or [[children]], held in place by [[ribbon]]s tied under the [[chin]].
# A traditional Scottish woollen brimless cap, also spelled [[bunnet]].
# {{context|Australia|British|automotive}} The [[hinge]]d [[cover]] over the [[engine]] of a [[motor car]]. Also called [[hood]] in other countries.
# {{nautical}} A length of [[canvas]] attached to a [[fore-and-aft]] [[sail]] to increase the pulling power.

====Synonyms====
* (''Scottish cap'') [[tam o'shanter]]
* (''cover over the engine of a motor car''): [[hood]] (''US'')

